        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Exposing humans to weightlessness during space flight induces significant structural and functional changes in the musculoskeletal system.  These changes are manifested as muscle atrophy and bone degradation accompanied by neuromuscular changes including muscle fatigue and weakness, abnormal reflex behavior, and diminished neuromuscular efficiency.  Recommendations to remedy the negative effects of microgravity on muscles and bones suggest that astronauts perform strengthening exercises while in space.  Such resistive exercises provide a load that is otherwise absent in space, presumably preserving musculoskeletal function.  Many principles must be considered while designing an exercise device as a countermeasure for muscle atrophy due to disuse.  Most importantly, load capabilities, constant force resistive output, and eccentric and concentric exercise capabilities should be the primary design goals of any resistive exercise device.<P>
The proposed experiment contributed to the development of practical and useful exercise countermeasures by introducing and evaluating a novel resistive exercise machine, the Constant Force Resistance Exercise Unit (CFREU).  Unlike past countermeasure devices, the CFREU has been designed to exercise muscle groups at a constant force concentrically and eccentrically throughout an entire range of motion during exercise.  The objective of this experiment was to evaluate the CFREU for ease of use, reliability, and life cycle in an International Space Station (ISS) analog setting.  Wireless physiological monitoring information was also collected during exercise.<P>
APPROACH:<br>
Prior to the mission, participants received a 1.5-hour training session on the CFREU mechanics, maintenance, and exercise protocols.  During the mission, participants performed a prescribed exercise protocol using the CFREU within the Aquarius habitat on selected days, and upon completion of each session, participants recorded results in an exercise log.  Crewmembers exercised in teams of two, over a one-hour time period.  Thirty minutes of exercise was performed by each crewmember; while one was exercising, the other acted as a "spotter".  The spotter was also responsible for ensuring that a video recorder was set up and each exercise session recorded. During each exercise session, one of the two participants wore a VitalSense physiological monitoring system.  This experiment evaluated the ease of use, overall mechanical performance, and maintenance issues related to the CFREU.<P>
RESULTS:<br>
NEEMO 6 Crewmember feedback was overwhelmingly positive; a highlight of the mission. Crewmembers suggested reducing the size further than it is, with a few design changes. Hardware performance was not optimal. The crewmembers noticed increased friction in the system that developed over the mission duration, which restricted them to performing exercises at >15lbs.
        </div>

